In this lesson we'll look at three quick laws that make working with powers way easier. An exponent just means repeated multiplication — so three to the fourth is three multiplied by itself four times. When two powers share the same base, you can combine them without expanding. First, the product law: when you multiply powers with the same base, you keep the base and add the exponents. Think of it as counting all the factors together — if you have three a's and then four more a's, that's seven a's altogether. Next, the quotient law: when you divide powers with the same base, you keep the base and subtract the exponents, since dividing cancels out the common factors. And finally, the power of a power law: when a power itself gets raised to another power, you keep the base and multiply the exponents, because a bracket of five a's, raised to the second, means five a's written out twice, which is ten a's. One important warning — these laws only work when the bases match. For example, two cubed times three squared cannot be combined, so you just calculate it directly. Now let's see it in action.